<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Acadia</h1><p class="st-meta">1604 CE – 1713 CE · capital Port-Royal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony of the Maritime region, whose farmers built dykes on the Bay of Fundy and whose later expulsion by Britain became a defining tragedy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1604 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons">Pierre Dugua de Mons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> wintered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> Island, and in 1605 moved across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-fundy">Bay of Fundy</a> to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-royal-acadia">Port-Royal</a>. Raided by English colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1613, granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in 1621 and occupied by Scottish settlers from 1629, the colony returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1632 and slowly took root.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Acadia was thinly governed, and in the 1640s rival lieutenants <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-menou-daulnay">Charles de Menou d'Aulnay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-la-tour">Charles de La Tour</a> fought a small civil war. Settlers reclaimed salt marshes with dykes and sluices called aboiteaux, growing wheat and raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-royal-acadia">Port-Royal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pre">Grand-Pré</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaubassin">Beaubassin</a>. Close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>, members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wabanaki-confederacy">Wabanaki Confederacy</a>, and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> defined Acadian life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>English forces held Acadia from 1654 to 1670 and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-royal-acadia">Port-Royal</a> for good in 1710. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713 ceded the peninsula to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a>, and in 1755 the British began deporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/acadia.jpg" alt="Acadia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1604 CE – 1713 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port-Royal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pierre Dugua de Mons, Charles de Menou d'Aulnay, Charles de La Tour</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Acadie</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=acadia" aria-label="Acadia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3117 596 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Acadia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3437 852L3327 789M3437 852L5054 728M3437 852L4997 651M3437 852L3483 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3327 789h0M5054 728h0M4997 651h0M3483 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3437 852h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3437 852h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port-Royal. <a href="